  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates two related routes for converting metal–organic framework (MOF) precursors into carbon-based low-dimensional materials while preserving overall shape. In the upper scheme, a sheet-like MOF is heated by pyrolysis and transformed into a thin nanosheet. The inset suggests that the original ordered MOF lattice collapses into a graphitic or carbon network containing dispersed heteroatoms or metal-derived sites.\n\nIn the lower scheme, a rod-shaped MOF nanorod undergoes pyrolysis to form a carbon nanorod with layered, wavy internal structure. A second step, labeled exfoliation, then peels or unwraps this carbon rod into a graphene nanoribbon. The progression emphasizes a morphology-directed synthesis strategy: the geometry of the starting MOF template guides the geometry of the final carbon product.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that MOFs can serve as sacrificial, structurally tunable precursors for nanoscale carbon materials such as nanosheets and graphene nanoribbons. The key comparison is between starting from different MOF shapes—flat sheets versus rods—and obtaining corresponding carbon architectures after thermal conversion, sometimes followed by mechanical or chemical delamination.",
